The wording supports a potentially strong defence, but not an automatic one. On your account, documentation began immediately after the patient was stabilised, which may satisfy “as soon as reasonably practicable.”

The panel should assess:

- the exact interval between stabilisation and the entry;
- whether leading the resuscitation made simultaneous documentation impracticable;
- whether policy required you, as team leader, to allocate a separate recorder;
- whether the entry was complete, accurate, timed, dated, and identified as retrospective if required;
- electronic timestamps, the resuscitation record, staffing records, and witness accounts.

A likely counterargument is that, even if you could not document while leading, you should have delegated documentation. Address that factually: explain whether a suitable person was available, whether roles were allocated, and why no separate recorder was assigned. Do not speculate.

You could state:

> The policy does not require documentation to be completed during the clinical event in every circumstance. It permits completion “as soon as reasonably practicable after the event.” During the arrest I was leading the resuscitation team and carrying out the responsibilities associated with that role. No other member of the team was documenting. I began the clinical note immediately after the patient had been stabilised, at approximately [time], and completed it at approximately [time]. My position is that this was the earliest reasonably practicable opportunity in the circumstances.

Use “immediately” only if the timestamps and timeline support it. If there was an interval, state its length and what you were doing during it.

My assessment: this wording gives you a substantive policy-based defence to an allegation based solely on delayed timing. It does not necessarily answer a separate allegation that you failed to allocate a recorder or that the completed record was inaccurate or incomplete. Your representative should focus the panel on the precise allegation and require it to identify which policy duty it says you breached.
